#### Axes.stackplot baselines
ok   stackplot(baseline='zero'): layer k spans [g0 + sum_<k, g0 + sum_<=k] (thicknesses preserved)
ok   stackplot('zero'): 'Constant zero baseline'
ok   stackplot(baseline='sym'): layer k spans [g0 + sum_<k, g0 + sum_<=k] (thicknesses preserved)
ok   stackplot('sym'): 'Symmetric around zero' -> g0 = -sum(f)/2 (ThemeRiver)
ok   stackplot(baseline='wiggle'): layer k spans [g0 + sum_<k, g0 + sum_<=k] (thicknesses preserved)
ok   stackplot('wiggle'): 'Minimizes the sum of the squared slopes' of the layer midlines -> g0 = -(1/m) sum_i (m - i - 1/2) f_i  [np.float64(-1.6666666666666667), np.float64(-2.0), np.float64(-3.3333333333333335)]
   Byron & Wattenberg's boundary-curve wiggle -1/(n+1) sum (n-i+1) f_i would give [-1.75, -2.0, -3.25, -5.0] (vs [np.float64(-1.6666666666666667), np.float64(-2.0), np.float64(-3.3333333333333335), np.float64(-5.166666666666667)]); the docstring does not say which curves
ok   stackplot(baseline='weighted_wiggle'): layer k spans [g0 + sum_<k, g0 + sum_<=k] (thicknesses preserved)
   weighted_wiggle g0 ['-2.0000', '-1.6250', '-2.3750', '-3.7639', '-4.0972', '-4.7222', '-6.0000']
   minimiser of sum_i f_i (midline slope_i)^2 ['-2.0000', '-2.3750', '-3.6250', '-5.2361', '-4.9028', '-3.2778', '-3.0000'] ; same objective with the layer order reversed ['-2.0000', '-1.6250', '-2.3750', '-3.7639', '-4.0972', '-4.7222', '-6.0000']
   weighted squared midline slopes: matplotlib 84.3542, minimiser 13.8819
FAIL stackplot('weighted_wiggle', float layers): 'Does the same but weights to account for size of each layer' (Byron & Wattenberg) -> minimises sum_i f_i * (midline slope_i)^2  matplotlib -1.6250 at x=1 vs -2.3750; equals the reversed-order minimiser: True
   weighted_wiggle with the same layers as int64: ['-2.0000', '-2.0000', '-4.0000', '-7.0000', '-7.0000', '-6.0000', '-7.0000']
FAIL stackplot('weighted_wiggle', integer layers): same baseline as the same values given as floats  [np.float64(-2.0), np.float64(-2.0), np.float64(-4.0), np.float64(-7.0)] vs [np.float64(-2.0), np.float64(-1.625), np.float64(-2.375), np.float64(-3.7638888888888893)]
